# Content Security Policy. Enforced.
#
# It shipped Report-Only with no report-uri for most of its life, which enforces
# nothing and reports nowhere. Making it real took three steps, each measured by
# serving this exact policy as enforcing and driving Convert, Compress, the PDF
# Editor and the docs page with real files:
#
#   8 violations  starting point; compressing a JPEG never completed at all
#   -> 6          inline `onload` handlers replaced by /async-css.js
#   -> 4          inline <script> blocks covered by build-time sha256
#   -> 0          `unsafe-eval` granted
#
# ## Why `unsafe-eval` is here
#
# Four violations were `eval`, from three ordinary JS libraries - ts-flp,
# font and turbowarp - not from the WASM engines. `wasm-unsafe-eval` permits
# compiling WebAssembly but not building functions from strings, which those
# libraries do at init.
#
# Refusing it would have meant dropping those handlers. Granting it costs the
# defence against injected code that evaluates strings; this app has no server,
# no accounts, no cookies and no session to steal, so that is a small loss.
# What it buys is `connect-src 'self'` **actually enforced** - the browser now
# refuses to send anything anywhere but this origin, which is the mechanical
# backing for "your files never leave your device", plus real object-src,
# frame-ancestors and base-uri. Four enforced directives beat five imaginary
# ones.
#
# ## The hashes
#
# `__CSP_SCRIPT_HASHES__` is replaced at build time by the `csp-hashes` plugin
# in vite.config.js, with one sha256 per inline script, computed from the final
# files in dist/. Two consequences worth knowing:
#
#   - Editing an inline <script> changes its hash. The build handles that; do
#     not hand-write one.
#   - A wrong hash does not fail loudly. It looks correct and blocks the script.
#     This bit once already: the placeholder is also named in this comment, and
#     a plain string replace took the *first* match, rewriting the prose and
#     leaving the header untouched while the build reported success. The plugin
#     now substitutes on the policy line only and errors unless it matches
#     exactly once.
#
# Moving those blocks to files was considered and rejected: the first sets
# `.dark` before the first paint, so an external script adds a round trip and
# can reintroduce the white flash it exists to prevent. Nonces are the other
# option and need per-request server generation, which a static host does not do.
#
# ## connect-src
#
# Kept exactly `'self'`. It previously allowed https://cdn.sheetjs.com, which
# nothing in the shipped bundle ever called - xlsx is bundled at install time -
# so the allowance was granting reach the app does not use.
#
# ## If you change this
#
# A CSP failure here hangs rather than errors: the original measurement had a
# JPEG compression sitting forever with no message. Re-run the enforcing
# measurement across all three surfaces before trusting an edit.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' 'unsafe-eval' __CSP_SCRIPT_HASHES__; worker-src 'self' blob:; img-src 'self' blob: data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
